## Introduction 

This is a summary report of [sales data](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/karkavelrajaj/amazon-sales-dataset) from Amazon India compiled by [Karkavelaraja J.](https://www.linkedin.com/in/karkavelraja-j-a758031b0/) 

The dataset contains more than 1,000 Amazon product ratings and reviews from 2023. 

<br><br><br>

```{r}
# Fx rates come from here[https://www.exchangerates.org.uk/USD-INR-spot-exchange-rates-history-2023.html], the average rate for 2023 has been used. 

usd_to_inr <- 82.5738

amazon <- read_csv("./data/amazon.csv") |> 
  rename(discounted_price_inr = discounted_price, 
         actual_price_inr = actual_price) |> 
  mutate_at(
    vars(discounted_price_inr, 
         actual_price_inr), 
    ~ as.numeric(
      str_remove(str_remove(., "₹"), ",")
      )
  ) |> 
  mutate(discounted_price = discounted_price_inr / usd_to_inr, 
         actual_price = actual_price_inr / usd_to_inr) |> 
  mutate(discount_percentage = as.numeric(str_remove(discount_percentage, "%")), 
         discount_percentage = discount_percentage / 100)

categories <- amazon |> 
  distinct(product_id, category) |> 
  mutate(product_type = str_extract(category, "[^|]+$"), 
         first_category = str_extract(category, "^[^|]+")) |> 
  mutate(sub_category = str_split_i(category, "\\|", 2)) |>
  select(product_id, category = first_category, sub_category, product_type)

amazon <- amazon |> 
  select(-category) |> 
  left_join(
    categories, by = "product_id"
  )
```


## Product types 

The plot below shows the most-rated (and antecedently, the most-purchased, given that all ratings require a purchase) product types on Amazon India. In-ear earphones are the most popular product type on Amazon India, followed by USB cables, smartphones and HDMI cables. 

<br> 

```{r}
amazon |> 
  group_by(category, product_type) |> 
  summarise(rating_count = sum(rating_count, na.rm = TRUE), 
            rating = mean(rating, na.rm = TRUE), 
            .groups = "drop") |> 
  arrange(desc(rating_count)) |> 
  mutate(pc = rating_count / sum(rating_count)) |> 
  filter(rating_count > 100000) |> 
  ggplot(aes(x = rating_count, 
             y = fct_reorder(product_type, rating_count))) + 
  geom_col(aes(fill = category)) + 
  scale_fill_viridis_d() + 
  labs(x = "Number of ratings", 
       y = "", 
       title = "Most popular products on Amazon India", 
       fill = "Category") + 
  scale_x_continuous(labels = comma)
```

<br>

Electronics, in general, seem to be the most popular category of products on Amazon India. This post-modern age does seem to require and generate an excess of electronic detritus. It would be interesting to compare this other online sales platforms there. 

The plot below provides additional detail and shows the 10 most popular product types by sub-category. As is expected, many sub-categories are dominated by one or two large product types, with a "long tail" of more miscellaneous options. 

<br>


```{r fig.height=8, fig.width=9}
amazon |> 
  filter(sub_category %out% c("Tablets", "PowerAccessories", "Laptops", 
