## Review content


```{r}
amazon_bigrams <- amazon |> 
  select(review_id, review_content, user_id, category, sub_category, product_type) |>
  unnest_tokens(bigram, review_content, token = "ngrams", n = 2) |>  
  filter(!is.na(bigram)) |> 
  add_count(bigram) |>
  separate(bigram, c("word1", "word2"), sep = " ") |> 
  filter(!word1 %in% stop_words$word) |> 
  filter(!word2 %in% stop_words$word) |> 
  count(word1, word2, sub_category, sort = TRUE) |> 
  unite(bigram, word1, word2, sep = " ") |> 
  bind_tf_idf(bigram, sub_category, n)  
  
```

Let us now take a look at the most common bigrams (two words) per product sub-category. 

These bigrams provide insight into what consumers believe to be the most relevant considerations when purchasing a type of product. 

For instance, in the Headphones and Earbuds sub-category, we see the that the top two bigrams are "sound quality" and "noise cancellation", followed by "1 hour" and "minutes approx", which are probably references to the charging speed of wireless earbuds. 



<br>

